   "tutor": "Here is the rule: for a plan, say am, is or are, then going to, then the plain verb.\nRight: \"The farmer is going to sell his sheep.\" Wrong: \"The farmer going to sell his sheep.\" The wrong one leaves out is, so the plan has no helping verb.\nLet me think aloud with one example: \"The pilot is going to fly to Rome.\" Step 1: the subject is the pilot, so I use is. Step 2: going to. Step 3: the plain verb fly, then the rest.\nNow your turn. Check the three parts and write the sentence correctly:\nCorrect: \"I going to study tonight.\"",
   "raw": "[[assess verdict=NOT_EVALUATED relevance=NO_ACTIVE_ITEM next=NEW_ITEM item=g1]]\nHere is the rule: for a plan, say am, is or are, then going to, then the plain verb.\nRight: \"The farmer is going to sell his sheep.\" Wrong: \"The farmer going to sell his sheep.\" The wrong one leaves out is, so the plan has no helping verb.\nLet me think aloud with one example: \"The pilot is going to fly to Rome.\" Step 1: the subject is the pilot, so I use is. Step 2: going to. Step 3: the plain verb fly, then the rest.\nNow your turn. Check the three parts and write the sentence correctly:\nCorrect: \"I going to study tonight.\"",
